' ============================================
' LEGEND
' ============================================
legend bottom left
  **Architecture: 3-Tier + Clean Architecture + Domain-Driven Design**
  
  **Design Patterns (Production-Grade):**
  • Service Facade Pattern (lib/auth.ts) - 11 functions, 28+ call sites
  • Interceptor Pattern (lib/api.ts) - Automatic JWT injection
  • Mediator Pattern (AgentOrchestrator) - 4 tools coordination
  • Repository Pattern (BaseService<T>) - Generic CRUD with RLS
  • Template Method (BaseController) - Standardized responses
  • Inheritance (Controllers) - BaseController → Agent/Dashboard
  
  **Critical Auth Flow (JWT + Supabase):**
  1. User signIn() → JWT saved to localStorage
  2. Hook calls apiClient.post() → REQUEST interceptor fires
  3. await getAccessToken() → extracts token from Supabase session
  4. Inject: Authorization: Bearer <token>
  5. Backend: extractToken() → validateToken() → req.userId
  6. Routes access authenticated req.userId
  
  **Data Flow Strategy:**
  • Commands (writes) → Backend API routes → Services → Database
  • Queries (reads) → Some direct Supabase (real-time, notifications)
  • CQRS-like separation for performance optimization
  
  **Middleware Pipeline (Order Matters!):**
  Helmet → CORS → Request Logger → Rate Limiter → Auth → Error Handler
  
  **Route Implementation Patterns (Pragmatic):**
  • Controller-based: /agent (AgentController → Orchestrator) ✅
  • Controller exists but unused: DashboardController (routes use inline) ⚠️
  • Service-based: /pdf, /scraper, /cron, /inbox, /ai
  • Inline DB: /email, /dashboard, /notifications, /search
  Trade-off: Speed vs Abstraction (both valid!)
  
  **Color Legend:**
  🔵 Blue: Frontend internal flow (Pages → Hooks)
  🟢 Green: Authentication & Authorization
  🟣 Purple: HTTP API requests
  🟠 Orange: External APIs (Gemini, Gmail, Web)
  🟡 Yellow: Middleware pipeline
  ⚫ Gray: Infrastructure (Logger, Cache, Config)
  🔴 Red: Unused code (exists but not imported)
  
  **Tech Stack (Production):**
  • Frontend: Next.js 16, React 19, TypeScript 5.6
  • Backend: Express 5, Node.js 20, TypeScript 5.6
  • Database: PostgreSQL 15 (Supabase), 17 tables, RLS enabled
  • AI: Google Gemini 2.0 Flash Experimental
  • Security: JWT (Supabase Auth), RLS policies, AES-256-CBC
  • Automation: node-cron, Puppeteer, Cheerio, nodemailer
  
  **Infrastructure:**
  • Frontend: Vercel (CDN, Edge, SSR)
  • Backend: Railway (Container, Auto-deploy)
  • Database: Supabase (Managed PostgreSQL + Auth + Storage + Realtime)
  • Monitoring: Winston logger, Memory profiler
  
  **Scalability Features:**
  • Rate limiting (global + per-route)
  • Response compression (gzip)
  • In-memory caching (LRU, 5min TTL)
  • Connection pooling (Supabase)
  • Request correlation IDs
  
  **Known Architectural Decisions:**
  1. EmailService exists but unused (Orchestrator uses inline nodemailer)
     → Reason: Cohesion, AI context integration, pragmatic trade-off
  2. DashboardController exists but unused (routes use inline Supabase queries)
     → Reason: Speed priority, simpler debugging, less abstraction overhead
     → Pattern: BaseController extended by 2 controllers (Agent ✅, Dashboard ❌)
  3. BaseService<T> only extended by UserContextService
     → Reason: Other services have domain-specific patterns (IMAP, CRON)
  4. Some routes use direct Supabase, others via services
     → Reason: CQRS-like separation, performance vs maintainability
  
  **Senior-Level Insight:**
  This is REAL production architecture, not textbook perfect.
  Trade-offs documented. Patterns used where beneficial.
  Pragmatism > Dogma. Speed > Over-engineering.
  
  **Database: 17 Tables with Row Level Security**
  User: profiles, email_configs, imap_configs
  Email: inbox, sent, templates, attachments, drafts, scan_logs, scheduled
  Documents: pdf_files, pdf_templates
  Automation: scrape_jobs, scrape_history, cron_jobs
  System: notifications, chat_messages
endlegend

